00:22:57,000 --> 00:23:00,220
at 2.04 p.m., the user clicked add to cart.

461
00:23:00,560 --> 00:23:02,520
Just a historical record of what happened.

462
00:23:02,640 --> 00:23:05,980
And this is where sphere pop introduces a radical paradigm shift.

463
00:23:06,500 --> 00:23:08,640
In this model, the event log is not passive.

464
00:23:08,920 --> 00:23:09,700
It is not a receipt.

465
00:23:10,020 --> 00:23:13,420
It is an active filtration constraint on the state space of the program.

466
00:23:13,580 --> 00:23:15,020
I love the maze analogy for this.

467
00:23:15,200 --> 00:23:20,440
Instead of a receipt, think of the event log as the literal walls of a maze you are running through.

468
00:23:20,620 --> 00:23:21,120
That's good.

469
00:23:21,120 --> 00:23:25,020
Every single time you make a choice, turn left, turn right, click this, download that,

470
00:23:25,240 --> 00:23:27,960
a massive spiel wall slams down behind you.

471
00:23:28,300 --> 00:23:34,020
Your history is physically, actively forcing where you are allowed to go next.

472
00:23:34,300 --> 00:23:35,760
That is a perfect visualization.

473
00:23:36,360 --> 00:23:39,640
The history of the computation becomes a geometric constraint on its future.

474
00:23:40,020 --> 00:23:44,060
You cannot revisit a state that your history has structurally blocked off.

475
00:23:44,060 --> 00:23:50,300
So what happens if a program tries to make a move that looks totally fine locally, like typing the correct password,

476
00:23:50,900 --> 00:23:55,220
but that action fundamentally violates the walls of the maze that have dropped behind it?

477
00:23:55,220 --> 00:23:58,780
That creates what the architecture calls a non-abelian tear.

478
00:23:59,440 --> 00:24:00,540
There's that term again.

